<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>netstandard2.0</TargetFramework>
		<!-- Neither RootNamespace nor PackageId: both default to the project name, which
		     is what they should be. Nothing else ships — everything a generated parser
		     needs is emitted into the consumer's own compilation (docs/syntax.md §6.1) —
		     so a .Generator suffix would separate this package from no other.
		     Folders give the namespaces below it: Grammar/ is the Roslyn-free half,
		     Generation/ is the shell. -->
		<Description>Source generator that compiles .gram grammars into C# parsers.</Description>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageTags>parser;parser-generator;grammar;source-generator;roslyn;analyzer;codegen;dsl;csharp</PackageTags>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>Since 0.2.0. A literal that ignores case ('x'i, "text"i) now means one thing wherever the generator asks — the match itself, first sets, the lexical automaton of a grammar with Lexical = true and the analysis of overlapping literals: each character matches itself and its simple upper and lower case as Unicode 16.0 gives them, and a case partner on the other side of ASCII is not taken. An ASCII letter matches exactly its two ASCII cases; "привет"i reads ПРИВЕТ; 'μ'i reads Μ but not the micro sign U+00B5; 'σ'i reads Σ but not ς. The generator carries its own table of those mappings, so the parser it writes no longer depends on the casing tables of the machine that built it (its ICU, invariant globalization mode, or .NET Framework in an editor). The generated parser tests those characters rather than folding at run time, except that a literal of two or more ASCII characters is one call to the runtime's ordinal ignore-case comparison, which on .NET 5 and later is exactly this rule. Behaviour change, after generating the parser again: an ignore-case literal no longer accepts a character that only upper-cases to one of its own — a one-character literal such as 's'i used to accept U+017F ſ, a literal beyond ASCII such as 'μ'i or "μs"i the micro sign, and 'σ'i final sigma; a literal of two or more ASCII characters ("select"i) already refused them and reads as before. A refusal no longer lists ſ among the characters such a literal expects. Before, the three places disagreed, so the same literal could read differently with and without Lexical = true. docs/syntax.md has the rule and a table of examples.

Breaking — the default carrier changed: GramCarrier.Auto, the default, now compiles every grammar it can with the immediate carrier (before: only where no construction could run for an abandoned reading). To get the old behaviour for a grammar, set Carrier = GramCarrier.Tape. What changes for a grammar that moves: constructions (=>) run as their alternative is read — also for alternatives the parse then abandons and for the prefix of a refused input; a factory with side effects, or one that throws there, behaves differently; an exception thrown there escapes Try* methods, and a throwing Parse* can surface it instead of the parse error; in a grammar with a context, constructions run interleaved with when guards, switch selectors and external recognizers that are handed the context: a guard or recognizer can see what a construction wrote, and a construction that reads context sees it as it stands at that point rather than after the whole reading — so both what the grammar accepts and the values of an accepted parse can differ; in a grammar with a context, a refused input runs constructions at least twice, and in a grammar with a context or a recovery, or in a rule whose own guard asks for its value so far, every way back the input takes builds again what stood before it — as often as the input gives ways back, not at most twice per loop (GRAM5016 says where); refused inputs are slower and allocate more (+10 to 29% measured); in grammars with a context or a recovery, or a rule whose own guard asks for its value so far, refused list input can allocate memory quadratic in its length; trace event streams and DOTGRAM_COUNTS output differ. Accepted parses are typically 30-60% faster and the generated code smaller. New warning GRAM5016 names a serious reason to keep the tape (context shared between constructions and guards or recognizers; quadratic refusal memory); GRAM5012 (information) names the others. An explicit Carrier silences both. GRAM5015 is no longer reported. A grammar the immediate carrier cannot carry is compiled on the tape; GRAM5007 warns only where Carrier = Immediate was named.

New: ConstructionsMayThrow = true on [Gram] or [GramOptions] says a construction (=>) of the grammar may throw on a reading the parse gives up — int.Parse, a division, a lookup that does not find. Carried immediately, what a construction throws is then held and thrown only where the tape would have thrown it: when the publication builds its answer, or where a when or switch asks for the value it was thrown under, from Try* and Parse* alike; an exception from a reading the parse abandons goes with it, and a refused parse throws nothing it held. Such a grammar can keep the immediate carrier and its speed and answer as the tape does, exceptions included. The hold costs every construction a handler and every way back a copy, whether anything throws or not, so it is off by default: a grammar that does not set it is generated exactly as before. The tape ignores it. GRAM5012 names it. docs/syntax.md section 7.5.

Fixed, on the immediate carrier: a left-recursive rule that builds nothing, beside a rule another calls, stopped the generator with "A value table for 'string' was named, and the file keeps none". Such a fold now writes no value.

Breaking: a with expression over the header of a namespace its operand calls into from outside is one header with it, and one that sends a name back to itself is refused (GRAM3011) — R = Ns.E with (D = B) under namespace Ns with (B = D), or (Ns.E with (D = Y)) with (Y = B). 0.2.0 may have compiled these, reading the operand under the header alone. Refused rather than read wrongly, until they are read as docs/syntax.md section 5.1 says: an argument of a call into a namespace with a header, written outside it, that names a rule the header rebinds — Ns.L(B) under B = D read D where B was written (GRAM4040, at any depth of the argument, through a parameter that passes it on, under an inherited header, and where a rebinding makes the call one into the namespace); and a with expression inside an argument of a parameterized rule, L(D with (D = Y)), which was dropped (GRAM4041). Before, both compiled and read the wrong rule. A with inside an instance made only while another rebinding was applied (GRAM4036) now names the instance, the call as written and the substitution that made it. Fixed: a with expression over another written on an operand of its own, ((X with (A = B)) &amp; '!') with (B = C), dropped the outer rebinding where the inner one had copied a rule — the parser read b! and refused c!; the outer now rebinds what the inner put in place, as docs/syntax.md section 5.1 says.

Fixed: a reader that runs its stack low carries the reading onto a thread of its own, and the reading it came back to went on counting entries from where the other thread stopped, probing the stack only on every fourth of them. Where one level of a recursion enters the probing rules a multiple of four times, the probe could fall on the same small readings beside the recursion at every level and never on the recursion itself, which then ran the stack out: ten thousand T-SQL CASE … ELSE CASE … ended the process. The reading now comes back with the count at zero, so every entry probes while the stack is still low. And every hand-off from one thread now goes to the same thread while it waits for another (a tenth of a second), rather than to a new thread each: a hundred thousand values read where the stack ran low were a hundred thousand threads and eight seconds, and are a third of a second. Regenerate to take the fix.

New: Std.Nested(open, close) in the standard library, a pair that holds others to any depth — Std.Nested("/*", "*/") reads /* a /* b */ c */ whole. In braced trivia and in a token the lexer begins it is read by a counter, which has no depth to run out of; everywhere else by its own rule, which says the same. Its arguments are two literals that cannot begin at one place, and anything else is the new error GRAM4037.

New: a trace build. Trace = true on [Gram] or [GramOptions], or the build property DotGramTrace for every grammar of a project (in a Debug build, say), compiles a parser that reports what it reads to a sink set with Tracing(sink) on the generated class, which every reading begun in the same flow of control picks up — one begun by a construction, one carried onto a stack of its own, one awaited in a task — until the scope is disposed. The sink is GramTrace, nested in the class and public where it is: Begin and End of each reading (End with the furthest refusal the reading recorded), Enter and Exit of each rule, Retracted for a rule whose success the engine takes back by going back, Refused with what a recording reading wanted, Guard, Remembered for a failure answered from memory, Recovered for an element a repetition marked recover stepped over, Deepened, Unlexed for a character no token begins with, and Rejected with the match's own message, worded only for a sink whose HearsRejections says so (GramProfile's does not). Three come ready-made: GramWhy, which says why the last call was refused — the match's message word for word, and the rules open where the input was followed furthest, each with the part of the set it wanted there, a guard by its C#, positions as line and column also over tokens; GramTraceLog, an indented log to a TextWriter within a budget of lines; and GramProfile, entries, successes, failures, remembered failures, re-reads of one position, inclusive and exclusive time and depth per rule, quiet readings apart from recording ones. A rule that only forwards another rule's value is compiled into its callers and reported where it stood all the same. A sink is the host's code, as a guard is: what it throws goes out of the call; and whatever leaves a reading by an exception, the sink is told every rule entered is left (Exit with GramTrace.Thrown) and the reading ended. A trace build answers what the parser answers; without the option nothing of it is generated, and a parser is the same text byte for byte. Every reading is traced — by methods, on the engine, by one flat method, over a string, a reader, a stream or bytes, and a parse handed over a part at a time — each reporting the rules it has a boundary for: the engine every rule it calls, a scanned rule around its scan, a flat method the rule it reads; a reading over a reader counts its positions from the beginning of the input. A character no token begins with is explained, where the reading writes into no context and the grammar runs nothing of its author's (no construction, guard or recovery factory), by the rules that were reading where the tokens before it ran out: with a sink set those tokens are read once more to say it. docs/syntax.md §6.9.

Fixed, on the immediate carrier: a repetition or a choice that gives a turn back re-reads what stands before it, and the reader built the standing turns again on every attempt — a list of a thousand members refused after its last one built half a million members and allocated 24 MB, four times as much at every doubling, where the tape allocates nothing. Once a loop has given a way back a second time, its further attempts now build nothing but what a guard names, and the attempt that stands is read once more and built: the same refusal builds the members a constant number of times — at most two attempts for each enclosing loop that gives back — and allocates 96 KB, twice as much at every doubling. The first give-back is read building as before — it is how a choice reads its next alternative, and an accepted address list gives one back for every member — so an accepted text with one give-back at a loop runs exactly what it ran. Values and messages are unchanged, over a string and over a buffered reader or stream alike. Visible to a host factory: on an attempt after a loop's second give-back it is no longer called, so one that threw there no longer escapes; what a parse that fails has run is at most two attempts per enclosing loop of what it read. Generated code of a grammar with a context or a recovery, or on the tape, is byte for byte what it was; GRAM5012 says what a failing parse may have run in these terms (docs/syntax.md §3.7, Carrier).

Fixed: a grammar that tells every rule's value where it was read (LocationType on the attribute, no per-call door) failed to compile, CS7036, where a rule built its value from its own captures alone: the rule was rendered flat, or its call compiled in place of it, and either way the call left out the range the construction is handed. Such a rule now keeps its own boundary in a located grammar and is told where it was read; the code written for every grammar that compiled before is unchanged.

Fixed: in a rule written with binding powers (&lt;&lt; and &gt;&gt;), a step of the operator loop could take an operator the operand before it had given back, reading the same text grouped the other way. Where the step has a when, that grouping was an answer the strengths exclude — left: E &amp; '*' &amp; when @(left.Length &gt; 1) &amp; right: E &lt;&lt; 2 accepted 1+2*3 as (1+2)*3 — and it is now refused. Over characters, where every rule can give back, each such operator was a second reading, so refusing a text cost two to the power of their count: the calculator of the README took two seconds to refuse 1/0+1/0+… of twenty terms, and now refuses five hundred and twelve in a tenth of a second. After an operand read at strength k, the loop takes only operators weaker than k (docs/syntax.md section 4.3.1). What a grammar accepts and builds changes only where a reading needed an operator the operand before it gave back: a when in an operator step, a when in a rule that calls the expression and reads its value (it is now handed only the grouping the strengths say), a lookahead after an operand that looks for the operator. A grammar read over tokens with no when in an operator step — T-SQL, SQL-92, the expression language — is generated byte for byte as before. Also fixed: a when after the right operand, left: E &amp; op &amp; right: E &amp; when … &lt;&lt; n, hid the operand from its strength, and it was read at 0, grouping to the right; it is read at the strength the marker says. New warning GRAM4042: a strength that cannot reach its operand, one inside an optional, a choice or a repetition. Breaking: a binding power is at most 9999 (GRAM2005); a larger one used to be taken, and one at the largest int read its operand at a negative strength.

Known limit: over characters, a repetition that gives a turn back reads the turns before it again from the start each time, so a refused text costs the square of its length there — a refused sum of 2,048 quotients in the calculator, about half a second. Bound the size of untrusted input.

Changed, and seen in an untraced parser: the engine's step trace, one line on standard error per step of the automaton for whoever works on the generator, is compiled where the build defines DOTGRAM_STEPS; it was DOTGRAM_TRACE. A project that defines DOTGRAM_TRACE gets no step lines any more after generating the parser again — define DOTGRAM_STEPS instead. DOTGRAM_TRACE now means nothing to a generated parser, so that the symbol a DotGramTrace user might guess does not flood standard error. The step trace is also written only where the build defines DOTGRAM_STEPS: a parser generated without it carries neither the trace calls nor the methods they call, which is tens of kilobytes less source for a large grammar and no difference at run time.

Fixed: a repetition marked recover inside another one's element or synchronization was read as a plain repetition, and the outer one stepped over the whole element in its place — ('[' &amp; (Word &amp; ';')* recover ';' &amp; ']' &amp; eol)* recover eol read [c;9;d;] as one bad line. It now recovers there as if it were a rule of its own, with the enclosing rule's value, trivia and on fail, on every carrier (docs/syntax.md section 8.2): it is made a rule of its own, so a guard inside reads what this reading of it captured. New error GRAM4045: what such a rule could not carry — a guard inside reading a capture made only outside it, a guard outside reading one made inside, a value built inside, a capture of a lookahead inside — is refused where the inner recover is written. Fixed: a recovering repetition in the synchronization another one looks for no longer moves where the outer search began. New error GRAM4044: with Lexical = true, a recover in a rule the lexer reads was read as a plain repetition too, and is refused where it is written. Fixed: a parse read from a TextReader whose one recovering repetition is not the repetition of a rule it hands back — Line* &amp; ('[' &amp; Word &amp; ']' &amp; eol)* recover eol — stepped over bad elements of the wrong repetition; it now gets no reader overload and is told why (GRAM5001).

New warning GRAM4043: a rule spelled from single characters where trivia is not empty — every operand a class, any, a one-character literal, eol or a call to such a rule, with trivia woven between two of them, as in Std.Digit+ &amp; ('.' &amp; Std.Digit+)? — reads 1 . 5 as a number and i f as a name. Write ~ between its parts, declare it where trivia = none, or name trivia in it where the space is meant. Runs such as Std.Digits or Word are not told (docs/syntax.md section 4.5). The Calculator, Filter and JSON examples now keep spaces out of numbers and out of the edges of a string; a grammar copied from the old Calculator reads 1 .5 as a number until its Point is joined with ~.

Fixed: a parse read from a TextReader or a sequence of lines could hand back other values than the same parse over a string. An ordinary repetition before the one marked recover was read as if it were marked, so Line* &amp; Row* recover eol lost its lines; a repetition's least and most were not held, and input left over after the last part was accepted. A rule that collects a value inside a part that is not a call, such as Line* &amp; ('[' &amp; Word &amp; ']' &amp; eol)*, or that builds its value with =&gt;, now gets no reader overload and is told why (GRAM5001) rather than one that drops those values; give the part a rule of its own. Without a host compilation (GramCompiler with its default symbol resolver), a rule building T[] is no longer collected as an element of a sequence of T, which wrote a parser that did not compile; it is refused with GRAM4008, as a build refuses it. A reader also reads the rule's trivia before and after it as the string does, gives no overload to a rule whose bounded repetition can begin what follows it (A? &amp; A+), and reports a refusal at the place the string reports it. A part that a choice of literals lets end in two places, such as ("a" | "ab") &amp; "b", gets no reader overload either (GRAM5001): the string tries the longer literal when what follows the shorter fails, which a stream cannot.

Behaviour change, after generating the parser again: a text capture of a repetition that may take no turn is "" when it took none, whatever the bounds, in a rule of one alternative or of several. c: 'a'{0,2}, (c: 'a' &amp; ',')* and a count by a length used to read null where another alternative of the rule, or an option around the run, leaves c unwritten; c: 'a'* already read "". Only ? (and {0,1}, which spells it out) is an option, null where it did not match. Over bytes such a capture was an empty span already, so the string and byte forms of one grammar now agree.

Behaviour change: a captured lookahead at a rule that builds a value holds that value, as docs/syntax.md section 3.4 says ?=X produces X's value: n: ?=Number with Number : @int is the int, of the rule's type, where it used to be the text the lookahead saw — and a grammar that handed it to a guard or a construction taking the rule's type did not compile (CS0029). A lookahead at a pattern, or at a rule whose value is its text, is still the text. The example of section 3.6 now declares its type, SmallNumber : @int, which a rule that builds with =&gt; needs (GRAM4008).

Behaviour change, after generating the parser again: \p{…} reads Unicode 16.0's general categories on every runtime, the version case folding already reads. The generator used to ask the runtime it ran on for the sets its analysis decides by, and the parser asked the runtime it ran on at every character it tested; the two could be different Unicode versions, so one commit generated different code on .NET 8 and on .NET 10, a parser built in Visual Studio, whose compiler runs on .NET Framework, could differ from one built with the .NET SDK, and the analysis could route a character away from an alternative the test would then have accepted. The generator now carries a table of Unicode 16.0 and reads only that, and the parser carries the bits of each set of categories it tests — about 2 KB for \p{L}, written only where a grammar names a category — and no longer calls CharUnicodeInfo. What changes: on .NET Framework, whose tables are Unicode 8.0, and on .NET 8 and 9, whose tables are older than 16.0, a character assigned or recategorised since — U+1C89 CYRILLIC CAPITAL LETTER TJE, a letter in 16.0, among them — is read as its Unicode 16.0 category, so a parser may accept a name the runtime's own char.IsLetter or Regex \p{L} refuses there; on .NET 10, whose tables are 16.0, nothing changes. A parser built in Visual Studio now reads categories as one built with the .NET SDK. A later Unicode version will come with a release of the generator and be listed as a behaviour change. Unchanged: a category is tested on a UTF-16 code unit, so a character outside the Basic Multilingual Plane is two units, each Cs, and \p{L} does not match it. docs/syntax.md section 3.1.

New error GRAM0010: a project that compiles C# below 8 — C# 7.3 is the default for netstandard2.0 and .NET Framework — and writes a [Gram] is told once, at the first, to set &lt;LangVersion&gt;8.0&lt;/LangVersion&gt; or later, and no parser is generated; it used to get a wall of compiler errors from the generated files. The attributes file every project referencing the generator gets, DotGram.Attributes.g.cs, is now C# 7.3 — no #nullable enable and no ? annotations — so a project with no [Gram], one that has the package only because something it references does, builds at any version. The attributes are the same; only the file's text changes.

Fixed: a rule that is told where it was written (LocationType) and builds a Nullable of a struct either did not compile where every rule is located (CS1061, no such property on Nullable) or, with PerCall, was boxed, told and handed back without its location. It is now refused where the rule is written, New error GRAM4046. A struct itself is accepted; docs/syntax.md section 6.8 says so. Fixed: with PerCall, a mutable struct that keeps what it is told lost its location, because it was told through a boxed copy; it is now told on the value the rule returns.

Fixed: a publication named input, or context in a grammar that declares one, did not compile (CS0149): the forms generated beside it that hand their argument on — the one taking bytes the caller holds, and the one taking a sequence of lines — call the publication by its simple name, which is their own parameter. They now call it through its class. The parameter names of the public forms are unchanged.

Fixed: a parse read from a TextReader or a sequence of lines, whose rule has a repetition marked recover, in a grammar that declares a context its constructions read, did not compile (CS7036): the reader form took no context and called the parts of the rule without it. It now takes the context after the reader, as the form over a string does, and hands it to every part it reads.

Fixed: an external recognizer or a predicate of an element set whose method is called text, p, c, failure, value, context and the like did not compile (CS0149): the generated method that calls it declares a local of that name. The call is now made from a method of the generated class whose parameters carry names of their own, so the method is found, and the overload chosen, as the author's C# would have found and chosen it.

Fixed: a repetition whose turn may match nothing only through an external recognizer — (@Read)*, or Frame* where Frame calls one — went round for ever where the recognizer answered true without moving the position, and where its turns built values grew until memory ran out. GRAM4001 takes a recognizer at its word that it consumes, and docs/syntax.md section 7.2 does not ask it to move. Now, in every reading, the turns up to a repetition's fewest are counted however far each moved, as the repetition written out turn by turn would read them, and past the fewest a turn that ends where it began is not taken: the repetition ends before it. So xs: V{3} over such a V holds three values, (@M){3,} reads M three times and (@M)* none. The same holds in a recovery's synchronization, and a count by a length is held to both its bounds. A repetition that reads something in every turn as the grammar is written is generated as before; one marked recover whose element may end where it began is read by the engine.

Fixed: where the input ended inside a literal of two or more characters, the refusal was Starved where the literal began, also where a character there did not fit it: T = "/*" &amp; ';' refused "x" and "/x" as input that ran out at 0, so a reader of a stream was told more input might help where none could. Now a character that does not fit is NoMatch where it is, and the refusal is Starved, at the end of the input, only where every character there agrees: "/x" is NoMatch at 1, "/" Starved at 1. And over a string, a rule read as one scan, such as W = (?!';' &amp; any)* &amp; ';', refused input it ran out in as NoMatch where the rule began; it is now Starved at the end, as over a reader or a stream. Behaviour change: such refusals now name the end of the input, or the character that does not fit, rather than where the literal or the rule began, in every form of the input (docs/syntax.md section 7.5). The same holds for a literal that gives way to what is tried next — an alternative after a run of literals, a longer continuation of one that matched, a literal after an optional or a repeated one — and for the last turn of a repetition read as a scan, the trivia's included: with trivia = { (' ' | "--" &amp; [^ '\n']*)* }, "-" is Starved at 1 and "- " NoMatch at 1 over a string as over a reader.

Fixed: a guarded repetition with a most, such as W = (?!';' &amp; any){0,2} &amp; ';', read over a string, accepted any number of turns: "abc;" was accepted where a reader refused it. And read from a buffer, a negative look over a run, such as T = ?!((?!'b' &amp; any)* &amp; 'b') &amp; any*, accepted "cab", which the string refuses: a failure after the look gave characters back into the run the look had read. Both now read as the other forms do.

A reader and a string give the same result for constructions without side effects. A construction that changes state, the context included, may run a different number of times in a streamed reading than over a string (docs/syntax.md section 6.3). New information GRAM5017 says so where a construction of a publication with a reader or lines overload assigns to, or increments, a member of context; it is syntactic and best-effort.

Fixed: a grammar with two or more publications and a repetition marked recover in a rule none of them reaches (GRAM4018 says so of such a rule) generated a parser that did not compile, CS1061 (no ExpectedMore on the failure record) or CS0649 (a Reach field nothing writes): the support code was written for a recovery that no machine of the file contained. A rule nothing reaches is dropped with what hung off its nodes, its recoveries included.

Fixed: a guard or a capture written directly in the synchronization of a recover — recover (when @(true) &amp; ';') or recover (c: ';') — stopped the generator with a KeyNotFoundException. Such a synchronization is now a rule of its own called where it stood, which is what a named synchronization already was and reads the same: a guard in it reads what the part itself captured before it, and a capture of it is read by nothing outside. A guard in it that reads a capture of the rule around it is refused where the recover is written (GRAM4045).

New error GRAM5018: a name that would be written twice into the generated class, said where the author wrote it — a publication called Match, Outcome, SourceSpan, ParserInput, OnRecovered, Failure or ParserEntry, the class or the type made for a rule (parse W as Match was CS0102 in the consumer's build); two rules that are written alike once their namespaces are joined with _ (A.B and a rule called A_B); two captures of one rule that become one property (a and A). Behaviour change, after generating the parser again: the type of a rule is made unique by the generator. The types the class nests for its own use are now named with _DotGram in them (Lines_DotGram, Parser_DotGram, Ways_DotGram, Window_DotGram and the rest, but Failure and ParserEntry, which are written so often that a longer name would be a share of the file), so find Word as Lines and parse Row as Parser, which were CS0102, mean what they say, and a rule with any of those names keeps it for its type. A rule named like Failure, ParserEntry, Match, Outcome, SourceSpan, ParserInput or OnRecovered takes Value after its name, and where that is another rule's name Value is added again: a rule that already has the name it wants keeps it, and the one renamed is the other. Rules called Window or Lines, which took WindowValue and LinesValue, are Window and Lines again. A rule called Grammar in the class Grammar and a rule called GrammarValue were two types of one name, CS0102.

Fixed: wordboundary = ['a'..'z']? with a use of word stopped the generator with a NullReferenceException. word is a run of the boundary, so a boundary that can match nothing is a repetition that does not end: it is now GRAM4001, said at the declaration of wordboundary.

New error GRAM1012: a name written in a grammar that contains _DotGram, which the generated code keeps for its own parameters, locals and the methods that call the author's external recognizers and predicates. GRAM5017 now sees (context.Rows)++, context.@Rows++, context!.Rows++ and skips comments. A parameter of an external method attributed CallerMemberName or CallerArgumentExpression gets the generated method's name and argument text. New: DotGram.Cli, a .NET tool (dotnet tool install DotGram.Cli) that runs this generator outside the build: dotnet dotgram generate writes the parsers a build would generate, for a project or for one .gram file, and the project compiles them without referencing this package. For large grammars, whose generation shows in cold builds; never both in one project. See docs/cli.md.</PackageReleaseNotes>

		<!-- The symbols inside the assembly rather than beside it. When the generator fails
		     in somebody else's build — GRAM0001, with the exception in the message — the
		     stack trace has line numbers without a symbol server to fetch them from. -->
		<DebugType>embedded</DebugType>

		<!-- Paths in the PDB and the package normalized to the repository rather than the
		     runner's disk, so that what CI packs can be rebuilt byte for byte from the commit
		     its nuspec names. Here and not in Directory.Build.props: it rewrites every source
		     path, [CallerFilePath] included, to /_/, and nine test classes find the
		     repository from their own file — set for everything, it failed eighty of them.
		     Only on CI, because locally it would point a debugger at /_/. -->
		<ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>

		<!-- Roslyn component: runs inside the compiler, ships as an analyzer. -->
		<IsRoslynComponent>true</IsRoslynComponent>
		<EnforceExtendedAnalyzerRules>true</EnforceExtendedAnalyzerRules>
		<IncludeBuildOutput>false</IncludeBuildOutput>
		<DevelopmentDependency>true</DevelopmentDependency>
		<!-- Expected for an analyzer package: it carries no lib/ assemblies by design. -->
		<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU5128</NoWarn>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- Optimized whatever the consumer's configuration is. An analyzer is built in the
	     configuration of the project that references it, and a Debug build of the
	     generator normalizes the SQL grammars with no JIT optimization and every
	     Debug.Assert live: the SQL parsers rebuilt in 56 s that way, 20 s this way, 16 s
	     in Release. The consumer's configuration changes nothing the generator emits,
	     and it should change nothing about how long it takes either. Stepping through
	     the generator itself is the one reason to want it otherwise, and that is what
	     `-p:DebugGenerator=true` is for. -->
	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug' and '$(DebugGenerator)' != 'true'">
		<Optimize>true</Optimize>
		<DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants.Replace('DEBUG', ''))</DefineConstants>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<PropertyGroup Label="Enabled Polyfills">
		<!-- netstandard2.0 is forced on us by Roslyn; these bring back the modern
		     types without adding a runtime dependency — Meziantou.Polyfill is itself
		     a generator and emits them as internal.
		     https://github.com/meziantou/Meziantou.Polyfill -->
		<Polyfill></Polyfill>
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.HashCode</Polyfill>
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.Index</Polyfill>
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.Range</Polyfill>
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis</Polyfill>
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.CallerArgumentExpressionAttribute</Polyfill>
		<!-- `required` needs both of these on netstandard2.0. -->
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.CompilerFeatureRequiredAttribute</Polyfill>
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.IsExternalInit</Polyfill>
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RequiredMemberAttribute</Polyfill>
		<MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>$(Polyfill)</MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- Pinned to the oldest Roslyn we intend to load into: a generator built
		     against an older Roslyn runs on newer hosts, but not the other way round. -->
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" PrivateAssets="all" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Analyzers" PrivateAssets="all" />
		<PackageReference Include="Meziantou.Polyfill" PrivateAssets="all" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The analyzer SDK picks these up as AdditionalFiles on its own; they only
		     need taking out of the default None glob so they appear once. -->
		<None Remove="AnalyzerReleases.*.md" />

		<!-- The standard library (docs/syntax.md §5.2) travels inside the generator: a
		     grammar that writes `using Std;` gets it spliced on, and there is no other
		     file for it to come from. -->
		<EmbeddedResource Include="Grammar\Std.gram" LogicalName="DotGram.Std.gram" />

		<!-- The writers of the case-fold and category tables are file-based apps (top-level statements, run with
		     `dotnet run`), not part of the generator: the default globbing would compile them in. -->
		<Compile Remove="Grammar/Model/CaseFold.generate.cs" />
		<Compile Remove="Grammar/UnicodeCategories.generate.cs" />

		<!-- The package's own README, not the repository's. The two answer different
		     questions: the front page argues for the project and the package tells
		     somebody who has already installed it what to write. It links back rather
		     than repeating what it links to. -->
		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="build/DotGram.targets" Pack="true" PackagePath="build/" />

		<!-- And the skill beside it, so that an agent working in a project that has
		     restored this package can read how to write a grammar without leaving the
		     machine. The README says where it is, in a comment nothing renders. -->
		<None Include="SKILL.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!-- Puts the assembly in the analyzer folder of the package. Done as a pack-time
	     target rather than a None item pointing into bin: a None item makes Solution
	     Explorer materialise a bin node even with Visible="false". -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>$(TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage);PackGeneratorAsAnalyzer</TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<Target Name="PackGeneratorAsAnalyzer">
		<ItemGroup>
			<TfmSpecificPackageFile Include="$(TargetPath)" PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/cs" />
		</ItemGroup>
	</Target>

</Project>
